  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>A defence services career India offers four real entry doors — NDA after 12th, CDS after
    graduation, the Technical Entry Scheme for PCM students with a JEE Main score, and Agnipath for
    other-ranks service — each leading to a genuinely different commitment, pay structure, and life shape,
    not one uniform "join the Army" path.</strong> Officers and other ranks follow separate ladders with
    separate pay levels and separate post-retirement outcomes, and the biggest decision most aspirants get
    wrong is not which exam to crack, but whether they have honestly tested their tolerance for years of
    postings, hierarchy, and family separation before they commit.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>NDA (after 12th) and TES (10+2, JEE-based) are the earliest officer-entry doors; CDS is the main route after a bachelor's degree.</li>
      <li>Agnipath recruits other ranks for a fixed 4-year tenure, with up to 25% retained for full regular service and the rest exiting with a tax-free Seva Nidhi.</li>
      <li>Permanent Commission means a full career and a pension after the qualifying period; Short Service Commission is a fixed 10-14 year tenure with no guaranteed pension unless you cross the threshold or convert to PC.</li>
      <li>Officer pay runs on the 7th CPC defence matrix (Level 10+) plus Military Service Pay of Rs 15,500 a month; other ranks get MSP of Rs 5,200 on Levels 1-9.</li>
      <li><strong>The real decision filter is whether you can sustain postings you do not choose, family separation, and a narrowing rank ladder above Lieutenant Colonel — not just whether you can clear the exam.</strong></li>
      <li>Use ECHS, DGR resettlement, and the ex-servicemen recruitment quota to plan your second career from day one of service, not your final year.</li>
      <li>Whichever route you choose, build a second, high-income skill portfolio alongside service — it is what protects your family's financial freedom if you exit at 4 years, at 14 years, or well before you reach the top of a narrowing rank ladder.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still comparing this against other government job tracks more broadly, read
    <a href="/blog/government-jobs/">government jobs guidance</a> for the wider decision context before you
    narrow down to defence specifically.
  </p>

  <p>
