  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer on a defence services career in India</h2>

  <p>
    Most people think of "joining the Army" as one decision. It is actually several separate decisions
    bundled together: which service (Army, Navy, or Air Force), which entry route (NDA, CDS, TES, or
    Agnipath), and which commission type if you are entering as an officer (Short Service or Permanent).
  </p>

  <p>
    Each of those choices changes your timeline, your pay structure, and — most importantly — how much of
    your life the service controls versus how much stays yours to plan.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Recruitment content tends to sell the uniform, the pay, and the pension in that order. The order that
      actually matters for a decision this size is: can you sustain the lifestyle for years, does the pay
      and pension structure match the commitment type you are choosing, and only then, which exact exam
      route gets you there fastest.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="officer-vs-ranks">Officer vs other ranks: the core split you need to understand first</h2>

  <p>
    Before comparing entry exams, get this distinction clear, because it decides almost everything else —
    pay level, training length, command responsibility, and life trajectory.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Officers</strong> are commissioned leaders — they hold command responsibility, sign for
    equipment and personnel, and are commissioned through NDA, CDS, TES, or other officer-entry schemes
    after written exams and an SSB interview. Officer pay starts at Pay Level 10 and above in the 7th CPC
    defence matrix.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Other ranks</strong> — soldiers, sailors, airmen, and Junior Commissioned Officers (JCOs) who
    rise through the ranks — form the much larger base of the pyramid. Since 2022, most fresh other-ranks
    recruitment runs through the Agnipath scheme rather than the older direct General Duty entries. JCOs and
    other ranks sit on Pay Levels 1 through 9.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      A capable soldier can rise to JCO and beyond through internal promotion and merit, but this path
      moves through a different ladder, pay structure, and timeline than the officer track. Decide early
      which ladder you are actually aiming for — the entry exam you prepare for depends on it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="entry-routes">Every entry route into a defence services career, in one table</h2>

  <p>
    Here is what each of the four main routes actually requires and how the process runs, side by side.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Who is eligible</th>
          <th>How selection actually works</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {entryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Who is eligible">{item.who}</td>
            <td data-label="How selection actually works">{item.process}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Age windows, exact eligibility percentages, and vacancy numbers change with every notification cycle.
    Treat the figures above as directional, current as of recent official notifications at the time of
    writing, and verify exact figures on the UPSC, Indian Army, Indian Navy, or Indian Air Force official
    recruitment pages before you apply.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nda">NDA after 12th: the earliest door, and the biggest early commitment</h2>

  <p>
    NDA (National Defence Academy) is open to Class 12 pass candidates roughly 16.5 to 19.5 years old.
    The Army wing accepts any stream; Navy and Air Force wings require Physics and Mathematics at 12th
    level. Selection runs through a UPSC written exam (Mathematics and General Ability), held twice a year,
    followed by the SSB interview and a medical exam.
  </p>

  <p>
    Selected cadets train for 3 years at NDA Khadakwasla across all three services together, then move to
    their respective service academy for 1 more year of specialised training before commissioning as an
    officer. That is a 4-year commitment before you even draw a full officer's salary — the longest runway
    of any entry route on this list, and the one that locks in the earliest.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cds">CDS after graduation: the degree-first route</h2>

  <p>
    CDS (Combined Defence Services) is the main officer-entry route for graduates. It requires a bachelor's
    degree in any discipline, and the eligible age band differs by which academy you target: roughly 19-24
    for the Indian Military Academy (Army), 19-22 for the Indian Naval Academy, 19-23 for the Air Force
    Academy, and 19-25 for the Officers Training Academy (Short Service Commission only, no automatic
    pension track).
  </p>

  <p>
    Selection again runs through a UPSC written exam plus SSB and medical, with training lasting roughly
    1 to 1.5 years depending on the academy before commissioning.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      CDS gives you several more years of adult life — including a full degree — to be genuinely sure this
      is the path you want, compared to committing at 17 through NDA. If you are unsure at 12th but curious,
      finishing a degree first while staying fit and CDS-eligible is a reasonable way to buy real decision
      time without losing the option.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tes">Technical Entry Scheme: no written exam, JEE Main-based</h2>

  <p>
    TES is a 10+2 entry specifically for PCM students who have appeared for JEE Main. There is no separate
    written exam conducted by the Army — shortlisting runs on your JEE Main score directly, followed by SSB
    and medical. Age eligibility mirrors NDA at roughly 16.5 to 19.5 years, and a minimum of 60% marks in
    the PCM stream at 12th level is typically required.
  </p>

  <p>
    Selected candidates get a fully funded 4-year B.Tech engineering education at an Army technical
    institution — three years of core engineering training followed by a year at the Indian Military
    Academy or a designated pre-commission academy — and are commissioned as Lieutenant once training
    completes.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-box">
    <p>
      TES appeals to PCM students who want an engineering degree without private-college fees and are
      already comfortable with a defence career. Because there is no separate written exam, competition for
      seats is intense purely on JEE Main score and the SSB stage — do not assume "no written exam" means
      easier entry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="agnipath">Agnipath: the other-ranks route now</h2>

  <p>
    Since 2022, most fresh recruitment into other-ranks positions across the Army, Navy, and Air Force runs
    through the Agnipath scheme. Eligible candidates are roughly 17.5 to 21 years old for the Navy and 17.5
    to 23 for the Army and Air Force, with a 10th or 12th pass requirement depending on the trade applied
    for.
  </p>

  <p>
    Selected Agniveers serve a fixed 4-year tenure, receiving full training, salary, and allowances during
    that period. At the end of the tenure, up to 25% of each batch is retained for full regular service —
    continuing under the standard other-ranks pay and pension structure — while the remaining 75% exit with
    a tax-free Seva Nidhi package (commonly reported around Rs 11.71 lakh) and a service-specific skill
    certificate.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Agnipath is genuinely the lowest-commitment way to test whether military life fits you, since the
      tenure is fixed at 4 years rather than an open-ended career. Treat those 4 years as an active test-and-plan
      window, not a guaranteed on-ramp to permanent service — plan a second-career track alongside it, not
      only after your exit date is confirmed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ssc-vs-pc">Short Service Commission vs Permanent Commission: the difference that matters most</h2>

  <p>
    For officers, the biggest long-term decision is not which exam you clear, but which commission type you
    end up in — and this is the part most aspirants understand least clearly before they commit.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Permanent Commission</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A full career until retirement age, with the widest promotion eligibility across the entire rank ladder.</li>
        <li>Pension entitlement after completing the qualifying period of service (broadly 20 years).</li>
        <li>Access to command postings and senior staff appointments that shape the highest ranks.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Short Service Commission</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A fixed tenure, commonly 10-14 years, with the option to apply for Permanent Commission after 10 years — vacancies are limited and not guaranteed.</li>
        <li>Officers who exit without crossing the pension-qualifying threshold and without a PC conversion generally leave without a service pension.</li>
        <li>Same in-service pay and allowances as PC officers during the tenure, including MSP and rank-linked pay.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Both tracks draw identical pay for identical rank during service — the split shows up entirely on the
    back end, in career length and pension eligibility, not in the monthly salary slip while you are
    serving.
  </p>

  <h3>Entry routes specifically for women</h3>

  <p>
    Women can join as officers through CDS-eligible OTA entries and Short Service Commission across most
    Army, Navy, and Air Force branches, including combat roles opened up in recent years such as fighter
    pilot and select arms in the Army. Agnipath also runs a dedicated Agniveer intake for women in the
    Navy. Eligibility, branch options, and vacancy counts differ by service and change with each
    notification, so check the current-year notification on the relevant service's official recruitment
    page rather than assuming last year's branch list still applies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rank-ladder">The rank ladder and how fast it actually moves</h2>

  <p>
    Rank progression for officers is not a smooth, guaranteed climb. The first several ranks move mostly on
    a time-scale basis; everything from Colonel upward runs through a competitive selection board with far
    fewer posts than eligible officers.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {rankLadderItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    JCOs and other ranks follow a separate but similarly structured ladder — Sepoy/equivalent moving up
    through Naik, Havildar, and into the JCO ranks (Naib Subedar, Subedar, Subedar Major) based on merit,
    seniority, and internal exams, staying on Pay Levels 1 through 9 throughout.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-perks">Pay and perks under the 7th CPC defence pay matrix</h2>

  <p>
    Defence pay uses the same 7th CPC pay matrix structure (Level 1 to 18) as civilian central government
    employees, but with a defence-specific Military Service Pay layered on top and a wider range of
    service-linked allowances.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Component</th>
          <th>What it actually looks like</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
