        {perksTableRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Component"><strong>{item.item}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it actually looks like">{item.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Exact allowance rates and DA percentages change periodically with government notifications. Treat the
    figures above as directional; verify current rates on the Ministry of Defence or respective service pay
    directorate pages before making a financial decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="post-retirement">Life after service: pension, health cover, and resettlement</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part most recruitment content glosses over, and it is exactly where the Short Service vs
    Permanent Commission choice, and the officer vs other-ranks choice, shows its real financial
    consequences.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {postRetirementItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Put together, these four pillars — pension where you qualify, ECHS health cover, DGR-run resettlement
    training, and ex-servicemen recruitment preference — mean a defence career rarely ends with a genuine
    financial cliff, even for personnel who exit relatively young. But none of these run automatically in
    your favour; you need to actively use resettlement training and quota exams rather than assume they
    happen for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    A pay table or a pension rule cannot tell you whether a defence career actually fits your temperament
    and your family's situation. Run yourself through The 4-Checkpoint Protocol honestly before you commit
    a training slot or a year of exam prep.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
